From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] Launcher3: a layout change arrives without taking the home screen apart

The default home screen is read once, into a database that was just created
(loadDefaultFavoritesIfNecessary), and nothing reads it again. So a release
that changes the layout had exactly one way to reach a device that already has
a home screen, and this project took it three times: move SCHEMA_VERSION, let
onUpgrade fall through to createEmptyDB, let the next load seed the empty
table (0011, 0012, and 0014 as it was first written).

What that costs is the whole workspace, because the table being dropped is the
only record of it: every icon the reader has moved, every folder they have
made, every application they have installed since the image was built. Which
was then answered three more times -- an entry for WeRead in the layout by
name, a rule that queued everything non-system back through the install path,
SCHEMA_VERSION and downgrade_schema.json kept in step -- and three answers to
the cost of one mechanism is the mechanism being wrong.

It is wrong because two unrelated things were one number. SCHEMA_VERSION is
about the tables: what the columns are, and how a database written by an older
release is read by this one. A layout is about what is in those tables on a
first open. Tying them together makes every layout edit a table migration,
with the migration of last resort -- start over -- as its answer.

So the layout carries its own version (pw3_workspace_layout_version in
res/values/config.xml), and what a newer version brings to a home screen that
already exists is the difference:

  * an entry already on the screen is passed over -- wherever the reader has
    since moved it, and whether it sits in a cell or inside a folder. Entries
    are told apart by the activity they open, or, for an entry that opens no
    activity, by its action. Not by the stored intent, which the layout parser
    and an install write differently for the same application;
  * an entry that is not on the screen is placed: in the cell the layout asks
    for while that cell is free, in the first free cell of the same screen when
    it is not, and not at all when the screen is full. The reader's arrangement
    is never moved aside for an entry of the image's;
  * nothing is removed. A row in this table belongs to whoever is holding the
    device; the layout is the floor of what the home screen has, not a mirror
    of it. Which also means that moving an entry from one cell to another, or
    taking one out of the layout, reaches a first open and nobody else -- the
    honest half of the trade.

Row 0 of the first screen is the search box, and it is not a row of this table:
the difference marks that row occupied exactly as LoaderCursor does, or an
entry placed behind the box would be dropped by the next load rather than
shown.

What this does not carry is a folder, a widget, a hotseat cell or a <resolve>.
None of them can be told from what is on the screen by the one thing compared
above, and placing one of them a second time is worse than not placing it at
all; each is logged and passed over, and the layout of this device has none of
them. tools/tests/test_home_screen_entries.py keeps it that way, and holds the
version number against a hash of the layout file so that an edit here cannot
move without it.

This runs before the workspace is read, on every device that installs the
release that moves the version, so anything it throws is a home screen that does
not come up -- and on this device the home screen is the whole interface. It is
therefore caught, logged, and tried again on the next load, which is safe
because the pass is idempotent: what it has already inserted is on the screen
the next time it looks, and is passed over. Nothing is placed where the reader
cannot see it either: the search for a free cell does not leave the screen the
layout named.

SCHEMA_VERSION stays at 29 and goes back to meaning what AOSP means by it. The
entry for all applications (0014) is what reaches an existing device this way
first.

diff --git a/src/com/android/launcher3/WorkspaceLayoutDifference.java b/src/com/android/launcher3/WorkspaceLayoutDifference.java
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/com/android/launcher3/WorkspaceLayoutDifference.java
@@ -0,0 +1,209 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+package com.android.launcher3;
+
+import android.content.ContentValues;
+import android.content.Context;
+import android.content.Intent;
+import android.database.Cursor;
+import android.database.sqlite.SQLiteDatabase;
+import android.util.Log;
+
+import com.android.launcher3.LauncherSettings.Favorites;
+import com.android.launcher3.LauncherSettings.WorkspaceScreens;
+import com.android.launcher3.config.FeatureFlags;
+import com.android.launcher3.util.GridOccupancy;
+
+import java.net.URISyntaxException;
+import java.util.ArrayList;
+import java.util.HashMap;
+import java.util.HashSet;
+
+/**
+ * PW3: what the home screen layout has that the home screen has not.
+ *
+ * A release that changes default_workspace_pw3.xml has to reach a device that
+ * already has a home screen, and the only mechanism this project had for that
+ * was destroying the workspace: SCHEMA_VERSION moved, onUpgrade fell through to
+ * createEmptyDB, and the next load read the layout into an empty table (0011,
+ * 0012). Everything the reader had done went with it -- the cells they had
+ * arranged, the folders they had made, the applications they had installed --
+ * because the table being dropped is the only record of any of it.
+ *
+ * So the layout carries its own version (R.integer.pw3_workspace_layout_version)
+ * and what arrives is the difference. This object is read from the table once,
+ * before the layout is parsed, and every insert the parser makes goes through
+ * {@link #wants} (LauncherProvider.DatabaseHelper.insertAndCheck): an entry
+ * already on the screen is passed over, one that is not is placed, and nothing
+ * is removed or moved. The layout is the floor of what the home screen has, not
+ * a mirror of it -- once a row is in this table it is the reader's.
+ *
+ * What it carries is a favorite or a shortcut in a cell of a screen. A folder, a
+ * widget, a hotseat cell and a resolve are passed over with a line in the log,
+ * because none of them can be told apart from what is on the screen by the one
+ * thing this object compares ({@link #keyOf}), and placing one twice is worse
+ * than not placing it at all. Such an entry reaches a device on a first open and
+ * no other way, and tools/tests/test_home_screen_entries.py keeps the layout to
+ * what this carries.
+ */
+class WorkspaceLayoutDifference {
+
+    private static final String TAG = "PW3Layout";
+
+    /** The key ({@link #keyOf}) of everything in the table, folders included. */
+    private final HashSet<String> mOnTheScreen = new HashSet<>();
+    /** The cells taken on each screen the workspace has. */
+    private final HashMap<Long, GridOccupancy> mOccupancy = new HashMap<>();
+    private final ArrayList<Long> mScreens = new ArrayList<>();
+    private final int mColumns;
+    private final int mRows;
+
+    private WorkspaceLayoutDifference(int columns, int rows) {
+        mColumns = columns;
+        mRows = rows;
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * Reads the home screen as it stands, or returns null when there is no
+     * screen to place anything on -- a workspace without a single screen row is
+     * not one an entry can be added to, and FIRST_SCREEN_ID is never removed
+     * (Workspace), so this says the table is not what it should be.
+     */
+    static WorkspaceLayoutDifference read(Context context, SQLiteDatabase db) {
+        InvariantDeviceProfile profile = LauncherAppState.getIDP(context);
+        WorkspaceLayoutDifference difference =
+                new WorkspaceLayoutDifference(profile.numColumns, profile.numRows);
+        try (Cursor c = db.query(WorkspaceScreens.TABLE_NAME,
+                new String[] {WorkspaceScreens._ID}, null, null, null, null,
+                WorkspaceScreens.SCREEN_RANK)) {
+            while (c.moveToNext()) {
+                difference.addScreen(c.getLong(0));
+            }
+        }
+        if (difference.mScreens.isEmpty()) {
+            Log.e(TAG, "the workspace has no screen to place anything on");
+            return null;
+        }
+        try (Cursor c = db.query(Favorites.TABLE_NAME,
+                new String[] {Favorites.CONTAINER, Favorites.SCREEN, Favorites.CELLX,
+                        Favorites.CELLY, Favorites.SPANX, Favorites.SPANY, Favorites.INTENT},
+                null, null, null, null, null)) {
+            while (c.moveToNext()) {
+                // Every row of the table, whichever container it is in: an
+                // application the reader has dragged into a folder is on the
+                // home screen, and putting a second copy of it in a cell
+                // because the layout names it is not an improvement.
+                String key = keyOf(c.getString(6));
+                if (key != null) {
+                    difference.mOnTheScreen.add(key);
+                }
+                GridOccupancy occupancy = c.getLong(0) == Favorites.CONTAINER_DESKTOP
+                        ? difference.mOccupancy.get(c.getLong(1)) : null;
+                if (occupancy != null) {
+                    occupancy.markCells(c.getInt(2), c.getInt(3),
+                            Math.max(1, c.getInt(4)), Math.max(1, c.getInt(5)), true);
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        return difference;
+    }
+
+    private void addScreen(long id) {
+        GridOccupancy occupancy = new GridOccupancy(mColumns, mRows);
+        if (id == Workspace.FIRST_SCREEN_ID) {
+            // Row 0 of the first screen is the search box, which is not a row
+            // of this table. LoaderCursor marks the same row for the same
+            // reason: an entry placed behind the box is dropped the next time
+            // the workspace is loaded rather than shown.
+            occupancy.markCells(0, 0, mColumns, 1, FeatureFlags.QSB_ON_FIRST_SCREEN);
+        }
+        mScreens.add(id);
+        mOccupancy.put(id, occupancy);
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * Whether this entry of the layout is missing from the home screen, and if
+     * it is, where it goes: the cell the layout asks for when that cell is free,
+     * and the first free cell of the same screen when it is not. The reader's
+     * own arrangement is never moved aside to make room for an entry of the
+     * image's; an entry that finds no room at all is not placed, and said so in
+     * the log.
+     *
+     * Changes {@code values} when it places the entry somewhere else, which is
+     * what the parser is about to insert.
+     */
+    boolean wants(ContentValues values) {
+        Long container = values.getAsLong(Favorites.CONTAINER);
+        if (container == null || container != Favorites.CONTAINER_DESKTOP) {
+            Log.w(TAG, "only a first open places this entry: it is not in a cell of a screen "
+                    + "but in container " + container);
+            return false;
+        }
+        String key = keyOf(values.getAsString(Favorites.INTENT));
+        if (key == null) {
+            Log.w(TAG, "only a first open places this entry: there is nothing to tell it apart "
+                    + "from what is on the screen (a folder, or a widget)");
+            return false;
+        }
+        if (mOnTheScreen.contains(key)) {
+            return false;
+        }
+        Long screen = values.getAsLong(Favorites.SCREEN);
+        GridOccupancy occupancy = screen == null ? null : mOccupancy.get(screen);
+        if (occupancy == null) {
+            // The layout asks for a screen this workspace has not got. The
+            // first screen is the one a reader is looking at.
+            screen = mScreens.get(0);
+            occupancy = mOccupancy.get(screen);
+            values.put(Favorites.SCREEN, screen);
+        }
+        int x = number(values, Favorites.CELLX, 0);
+        int y = number(values, Favorites.CELLY, 0);
+        int spanX = Math.max(1, number(values, Favorites.SPANX, 1));
+        int spanY = Math.max(1, number(values, Favorites.SPANY, 1));
+        if (!occupancy.isRegionVacant(x, y, spanX, spanY)) {
+            int[] vacant = new int[2];
+            if (!occupancy.findVacantCell(vacant, spanX, spanY)) {
+                Log.w(TAG, "screen " + screen + " is full; " + key + " is not placed");
+                return false;
+            }
+            x = vacant[0];
+            y = vacant[1];
+            values.put(Favorites.CELLX, x);
+            values.put(Favorites.CELLY, y);
+        }
+        occupancy.markCells(x, y, spanX, spanY, true);
+        mOnTheScreen.add(key);
+        return true;
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * What tells two entries of this table apart.
+     *
+     * The activity a row opens, where there is one: the two writers of this
+     * table store the same application under different intents -- the layout
+     * parser puts no flags on it, an install puts FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK on it
+     * -- and the application is the same application. Where there is no
+     * activity, the action, which is what the launcher answers such a row by
+     * (Utilities.ACTION_ALL_APPS_ENTRY); it does not move when the flags around
+     * it do. A row that has neither has no identity here and is passed over.
+     */
+    private static String keyOf(String uri) {
+        if (uri == null) {
+            return null;
+        }
+        try {
+            Intent parsed = Intent.parseUri(uri, 0);
+            if (parsed.getComponent() != null) {
+                return parsed.getComponent().flattenToShortString();
+            }
+            return parsed.getAction();
+        } catch (URISyntaxException e) {
+            return null;
+        }
+    }
+
+    private static int number(ContentValues values, String column, int fallback) {
+        Integer value = values.getAsInteger(column);
+        return value == null ? fallback : value;
+    }
+}
